course: dictionary
lesson: 4574
title: Hell: a place and a rude word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word hell has two very different jobs. In stories and religious lessons, hell is a place: in some religions, it is where bad people are believed to go after death to be punished. It is the opposite of heaven.
explain: In everyday speech, hell is a rude word. It is a mild swear word, used to show anger or surprise, or to add emphasis. Polite talk and school writing do not use it. Ask yourself which meaning fits. Is the text talking about a place after death, or is someone just cross or shocked?
explain: The words around hell give you the clues. In «For hundreds of years people avoided saying the names of God, the Devil and hell out loud, because it felt rude or unlucky», the word hell is a place, and people avoided saying it. When you meet hell in your own reading, check the context before you decide.
fact: Hell means a place where bad people are believed to go after death to be punished, in some religions.
fact: In religious stories, hell is the opposite of heaven.
fact: In everyday speech, hell is a rude, mild swear word, so avoid it in school writing.
fact: «Heck» is a milder word used instead of «hell», as in «What the heck?»
exercise: «For hundreds of years people said «down below» instead of hell.» What does «down below» mean here? | A deep hole for rubbish | A cellar under a house | The bottom of the sea | *Hell, the place some believe bad people go after death
exercise: «What the heck?» is a mild way to say something. What does «heck» show? | *Mild surprise or anger, without being rude | A place of punishment | A very rude swear word | A church song
exercise: «The three-hour traffic jam was pure hell.» What does hell mean here? | *A very unpleasant experience | A quick, pleasant trip | A friendly party | A sunny holiday
test: Why do schools ask pupils not to use the word hell in speech or writing? | Because it is too long to spell | Because it is only used in maths | *Because it is a rude, mild swear word | Because it means heaven
test: «Beliefs about hell differ widely and are disputed.» What does this tell you? | Everyone in the world agrees exactly what hell is | *People in different religions and families hold different beliefs about hell | Hell is the same as heaven | Hell is a town in England
exam: Which meaning of hell fits «old stories say bad people go to hell after death»? | Mild swear word used to show anger | A kind of heavy rain | A grey, wet day | *A place of punishment after death, in some religions
